<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="58308" href="schedule/2011/fall/CS/598/58308.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2011" href="schedule/2011.xml">2011</calendarYear><term id="120118" href="schedule/2011/fall.xml">Fall 2011</term><subject id="CS" href="schedule/2011/fall/CS.xml">Computer Science</subject><course id="598" href="schedule/2011/fall/CS/598.xml">Special Topics</course></parents><sectionNumber>RAR</sectionNumber><creditHours>4 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Topic: Probabilistic Graphical Models for AI. Probabilistic graphical models underly many critical representation, reasoning and inference tasks in modern AI.  They come in many flavors, from  Bayesian Nets to Markov Random Fields.  There is now a coherent body of work on how to  learn them, interrogate them, and optimize them.  We will survey the field, along with relevant background on probability and optimization.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2011-08-22Z</startDate><endDate>2011-12-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="S10">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>03:30 PM</start><end>04:45 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>112</roomNumber><buildingName>Transportation Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Rutenbar" firstName="R">Rutenbar, R</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>